WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
The shared responsibility model is a practical starting point for cloud incident response.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:10.020
It clarifies which responsibilities generally belong to the cloud provider and which

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.760
belong to the customer.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.980
The provider is typically responsible for the security of the underlying cloud

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.240
infrastructure.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.580
The customer is typically responsible for how accounts, identities, workloads, networks,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.720
storage, data, and applications are configured and used.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.660
The exact boundary changes by service type.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:34.200
Infrastructure as a service gives customers more direct responsibility for operating

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.380
systems,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.040
network rules, workload configuration, and patching.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:44.140
Platform services shift some operational responsibility to the provider but still require

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.040
secure configuration,

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.540
identity control, data protection, and monitoring.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.920
Serverless, containers, managed databases, identity providers,

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:58.940
and SaaS platforms each change what responders can directly inspect or change.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:05.080
Scope is also defined by account, subscription, project, tenant, region, environment,

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.820
and service ownership.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.840
A security team may have access to central logs but not to every workload account.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.860
A development team may own an application subscription.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.200
A SaaS administrator may control audit exports.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.100
During an incident, responders need to know where authority begins,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.400
where escalation is required, and which teams own the affected services.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.420
Misunderstanding responsibility can slow response.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.040
If the customer assumes the provider will detect every exposed storage object,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.020
important alerts may be missed.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.760
If the responder assumes they can acquire evidence the provider controls,

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.660
the team may waste time instead of opening the right support or legal channel.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.480
Clear responsibility mapping helps set realistic response actions.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.420
A strong cloud response plan identifies what the team can control directly,

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.720
what must be coordinated with service owners, what requires provider support,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:01.580
and what requires legal, privacy, or compliance coordination.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.480
This prevents responders from improvising authority boundaries during a high-pressure

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:06.580
event.
